  "meta": {
    "title": "Kelp Forest Wrack: Alginate Grazers of the Giant Kelp Holdfast",
    "date": "2026-08-23",
    "lesson": "When giant kelp (Macrocystis pyrifera) fronds break off in a storm and drift down to the forest floor, they don't just sit there rotting passively -- they become a substrate war. Kelp cell walls are built from alginate and laminarin, algal polysaccharides that most marine bacteria can't touch. A specialized guild of copiotrophic bacteria (Alteromonas, Zobellia, Saccharicrinis-type Bacteroidetes) carries the rare alginate lyases and laminarinases needed to crack these polymers, releasing sugars that feed a much larger community of opportunists riding along on the wrack. This level asks you to be one of those specialists: your founder needs real alginate-lyase and laminarinase activity (carbohydrate class dominant) to break into a fresh frond, while protease helps you scavenge the fouling animal and epiphyte debris tangled in the blades. Kelp wrack sinks through a shallow, cool, turbulent kelp-forest water column with a diatom epiphyte community fouling the blades near the surface and heavier detritus persisting longer in the dim water below. Twitching motility matters here because kelp wrack is a physical raft you can grip and ride while you dig into it, rather than a diffuse cloud, so twitching is boosted; but gripping a particle also means a phage can find you more easily on it, which is why twitching hosts are extra exposed to viral hunters in the kelp-fall guild.",
    "citation": "Kelp-fall decomposition and specialist polysaccharide-degrading bacterial guilds, e.g. Alteromonas/Zobellia alginolytic succession studies (temperate Pacific kelp forests).",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Giant kelp (Macrocystis pyrifera) forest, detrital wrack decomposition, temperate Pacific coast (e.g. central California kelp forest floor, ~15-30m depth)"
  },
